# spline_cap_scallop.cam — constant-scallop over a spline meridian (the
# follow-up spline_cap.cam's waterline slice named; DECISIONS.md "Constant
# scallop over a spline meridian…"). Same cap, same meridian.spline (a verbatim
# copy — each .cam fixture stays self-contained), but spaced by the family
# Newton march (parse::derive_spline_revolve_step_u) instead of uniform arc
# length: the cusp triangle is picked per march point from the meridian's exact
# signed curvature (convex/concave/flat), so the cusp height stays at the
# requested scallop everywhere instead of drifting with the curvature.
#
# scallop=0.05 with the 6mm ball gives a chord ≈ 2·sqrt(2·3·0.05 − 0.0025)
# ≈ 1.09mm over the ~25mm meridian, ~25 rings. The apex ring is scallop-
# invariant (the march starts at u0 with the same end-tangent CL point as the
# waterline: X50.949, Z22.846) — the hand-derived anchor the golden test pins.
#
# CONTAINED 2026-08-13 (DECISIONS.md "rim-area accounting…", slice 2):
# the stock used to stop AT this feature's base plane, so the whole
# surface stood in air and the fixture only ran because there was
# nothing to hit. The stock now stands 2mm proud of the feature's top
# (z 22) and T1 roughs the moat before the finish;
# numbers in the older comments describe the same toolpath, whose
# motion is unchanged where it touches the surface.
units mm
feed 600
plunge 200
rapid_z 25
clearance_z 40
spindle 4000

stock Box(w=100, l=80, h=62, x=0, y=0, z=22)

tool T1 Endmill(dia=12.0)
tool T10 Ballmill(dia=6.0)

# The spline cap sits centred at (50, 40); z=0 places its base rim on z=0, apex
# at z=20 (the meridian's own z, added to the feature z).
feature Cap SplineRevolve(file="meridian.spline", x=50, y=40, z=0)

run Clear(tool=T1, profile=Cap, stepover=8.0, stepdown=5.0, stock_to_leave=0.5)
run Copy(tool=T10, surface=Cap, scallop=0.05)
